---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Privacy, in plain English — Trout';
const description =
  'No accounts, no location tracking, no third-party trackers, no cookies. Trout is offline-first, so the strongest privacy policy is the architecture itself. Here is exactly what leaves your device.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/privacy/">
  <h1>Privacy, in plain English</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Trout keeps your fishing notes and planning data on your device. Optional notifications and
    corrections use the server only when you choose those features.
  </p>

  <h2>What leaves your device</h2>
  <p>Public browsing and optional submissions have different data needs:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The app requests public data:</strong> USGS gauge readings and the weekly stocking
      schedule are fetched as public, pre-generated JSON files. Those requests contain no account
      identifier (there are none), no precise coordinates, and no device fingerprint.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shop reports you read</strong> load from the same public snapshots, attributed to the
      shop that wrote them.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Opt-in water watches.</strong> Enabling notifications sends your browser's push endpoint,
      encryption keys, and chosen water/rules to Trout. Notices travel through your browser's push
      provider. Settings lets you remove rules or unsubscribe; inactive subscriptions expire after
      90 days. Permission alone does not guarantee delivery.</li>
    <li><strong>Water corrections you submit.</strong> Trout stores the proposed change, supporting
      source and context for moderation. Do not include contact details, private fishing spots, or
      sensitive information. Your receipt is the credential for checking its status. Closed records
      are removed after 90 days; submissions awaiting review remain in the queue. Moderator notes
      shown with your receipt are public to its holder.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What never leaves your device</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your location.</strong> "Nearest stream" is computed on your device from the stream
      catalog. Your coordinates are never included in any request — this is enforced by an automated
      privacy test in our QA suite, not just by policy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your logbook.</strong> Catch notes live in your browser's local storage (IndexedDB).
      They are never synced or uploaded. Photos, saved waters, groups and trip plans also stay local.
      An explicit JSON/full-photo backup downloads a file you control; sharing that file is your choice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bug identifications.</strong> The match-the-hatch key is pure, offline logic — your
      observation never travels.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No accounts in v1 — nothing to sign up for, nothing to leak.</li>
    <li>No advertising or behavioral tracking. Cookie-free Cloudflare aggregate analytics can be
      enabled at build time; builds without that configuration load no analytics beacon.</li>
    <li>No cookies on this site; the app stores your offline data in your browser, locally.</li>
    <li>We do not sell visitor data.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How you can verify this</h2>
  <p>
    Turn on airplane mode: the hatch key, ID flow, hatch charts, and last-known conditions keep
    working. A privacy-audit test in our public QA suite intercepts every network request the app
    makes during ordinary browsing and checks that coordinates are not uploaded. Optional notification
    enrollment uses your browser's push service; opening external directions or source links is your choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Optional aggregate analytics</h2>
  <p>
    The app supports a build-time Cloudflare Web Analytics opt-in. It uses no cookies or visitor
    fingerprinting. The operator can disable it by rebuilding without the analytics configuration.
    Network access logs may still be retained by hosting/proxy infrastructure; the app logger removes
    watch capabilities and receipt codes from request URLs and does not log authorization headers.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this page? See <a href="/about/">About</a> for how the project is run. This page
    describes v1 and lives in the same repository as the code it describes, so it can be audited
    alongside the app.
  </p>
</Base>
